From 739bebcbcfcd0d38642db3e3ce05dc8409bfb76d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ashfordeOU <236818772+ashfordeOU@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:05:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] C7 step 4: ANISE, the fourth (file-based) tool, evaluates
 Kshana's SPK and its own conversion of the OEM at every node, no further from
 the written states than SPICE

integrations/flight-dynamics/anise_reader.py and anise.ipynb (.md mirror): ANISE 0.10.6
reads the SPK with its own reader (not the SPICE Toolkit) and converts the OEM to a type 9
kernel; its Python API evaluates rather than hands back records, so both are evaluated at
the 4,321 node epochs and compared with the written states and with SPICE's own evaluation
of Kshana's SPK (spiceypy 8.3.0): within 1.46e-11 km and 4.94e-15 km/s (SPK) and
1.46e-11 km, 1.11e-16 km/s (OEM), against SPICE's 1.46e-11 km and 4.97e-15 km/s.

Kshana's SPK is byte for byte the kernel SPICE's spkw13 writes from the same states
(checked once outside the repository). ANISE reads SPK epochs as SPICE ET and the OEM's
TDB labels with hifitime's TDB model, 3.2 us apart at the first node; evaluating the SPK
on the wrong scale moves the states by up to 3.3e-6 km, which the notebook shows.

kshana_fd.py: evaluated_compare and the "evaluated, not read" rows of the report (carried
when every epoch is the written one and no state is further from the written one than
SPICE's evaluation). The workflow's tool matrix gains anise.
